ealth issue\n\nOropharyngeal dysphagia is a serious condition that affects a patient's ability to swallow, leading to complications such as malnutrition and aspiration pneumonia. This condition is prevalent among patients with various medical issues, particularly those who have suffered strokes or other neurological disorders. The complexity of managing oropharyngeal dysphagia necessitates a comprehensive approach involving dietary modifications, rehabilitation techniques, and specialized nursing care to ensure safe swallowing and prevent further health complications. [Data: Entities (7); Relationships (115, 117, 131, 208, 26, +more)]\n\n## Role of healthcare professionals in managing dysphagia\n\nHealthcare professionals, including nurses and physicians, play a vital role in the assessment and management of oropharyngeal dysphagia. Notable figures such as Lourdes Moneva Vicente and Mónica Benito Fernández are dedicated to providing nutritional treatment and care for patients with this condition. Their involvement in clinical practice and research highlights the importance of a multidisciplinary approach to improve patient outcomes. These professionals are also referenced in key guidelines and manuals that address the management of dysphagia, underscoring their expertise in this area. [Data: Entities (41, 33); Relationships (1104, 1087, 1102, 1106, 1107, +more)]\n\n## Importance of dietary adaptations for dysphagia patients\n\nDietary adaptations are crucial for patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia to ensure safe swallowing and adequate nutrition. The development of modified texture diets, such as traditional and commercial options, is essential for meeting the specific needs of these patients. These dietary modifications help prevent complications like aspiration pneumonia and malnutrition, which are common among individuals with swallowing difficulties. The relationship between dysphagia and nutritional support is significant, as proper dietary management can greatly enhance patient quality of life. [Data: Entities (929, 928, 675); Relationships (170, 175, 211, 107, 106, +more)]\n\n## Research and guidelines on dysphagia management\n\nResearch plays a critical role in advancing the understanding and management of oropharyngeal dysphagia. Various studies and guidelines have been developed to provide healthcare professionals with effective strategies for assessment and treatment. The 'Manual de Disfagia Orofaringea' serves as a comprehensive resource, offering insights into rehabilitation techniques and dietary management. Contributions from specialists like Carmen Gómez Triguero and Natasha Pérez Marrero further enhance the knowledge base in this field, ensuring that patients receive evidence-based care. [Data: Entities (39, 42); Relationships (128, 1102, 1106, 1107, 1198, +more)]\n\n## Technological advancements in dysphagia management\n\nTechnological advancements, including telemedicine and big data analytics, are increasingly being utilized to manage oropharyngeal dysphagia. These innovations facilitate remote assessments and interventions, allowing healthcare providers to monitor patients effectively and tailor treatment plans to individual needs. The integration of technology in dysphagia management not only improves access to care but also enhances the overall quality of treatment, making it a vital component of modern healthcare practices. [Data: Entities (191); Relationships (190, 192)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n300,VIDEOFLOUROSCOPIA,\"VIDEOFLOUROSCOPIA is a diagnostic imaging technique that plays a crucial role in evaluating swallowing function, particularly in patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia. This method allows for the observation of the swallowing process through real-time X-ray imaging, providing both lateral and anteroposterior projections. It is considered the gold standard for diagnosing dysphagia, as it comprehensively studies the three phases of swallowing. During the procedure, barium is used to enhance visualization, enabling healthcare professionals to assess the pharyngeal phase of swallowing effectively. Videofluoroscopy is particularly recommended when other assessment methods, such as Fiberoptic Endoscopic Evaluation of Swallowing (FEES), are not feasible. Overall, videofluoroscopy is an essential tool in the diagnosis and management of swallowing disorders.\",11\r\n38,NURIA GIL-FOURNIER ESQUERRA,\"Nuria Gil-Fournier Esquerra is a Specialist Physician in the Endocrinology and Nutrition Section at the Hospital Universitario Príncipe de Asturias. She also practices at the Hospital General de Villalba in Madrid, where she specializes in endocrinology and nutrition. In addition to her clinical work, Nuria is a contributor to clinical guidelines on dysphagia, indicating her expertise in this area, which is closely related to otolaryngology. She works in the Unidad Funcional de Disfagia Orofaríngea, where she is actively involved in the treatment of patients with dysphagia. Furthermore, Nuria is a researcher focused on dysphagia screening methods and clinical assessments, and she explores the intersection of internet technology and health, particularly in relation to dysphagia.\",7\r\n383,VIDEOENDOSCOPIA DE LA DEGLUCIÓN,\"The \"\"VIDEOENDOSCOPIA DE LA DEGLUCIÓN\"\" is a diagnostic procedure that allows for the observation of the swallowing phase in patients, which is crucial for diagnosing oropharyngeal dysphagia. This technique involves both static and dynamic assessments of the anatomical structures involved in the swallowing process. It was first described by Dr. Susan Langmore in 1988 and is utilized to evaluate swallowing function and anatomy in patients experiencing difficulties with swallowing. The procedure is conducted in specialized units, such as the UFDOF unit, ensuring a comprehensive evaluation of the patient's swallowing capabilities.\",6\r\n486,ESOFAOGOSCOPIA TRANSNASAL,Esofagoscopia transnasal is